    (v_topic_id, 'A 13-year-old gymnastics player presents with posterior heel pain. The pain worsens after running and improves with rest. Examination reveals tenderness upon medial-lateral compression of the calcaneal tuberosity (positive ''squeeze test''). There is tightness of the Achilles tendon. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Plantar fasciitis", "Calcaneal stress fracture", "Calcaneal apophysitis (Sever disease)", "Achilles tendon rupture", "Tarsal coalition"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Sever disease is a traction apophysitis of the calcaneus where the Achilles tendon inserts. It is a common cause of heel pain in athletic children aged 8-12. The classic exam finding is pain with medial/lateral compression of the heel. Treatment includes heel cups, calf stretching, and NSAIDs.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 14-year-old basketball player complains of anterior knee pain that is worse after practice and jumping. Examination reveals point tenderness and a bony prominence over the tibial tubercle. Radiographs show fragmentation of the tibial tubercle apophysis. What is the most appropriate management?', '["Activity modification, ice, NSAIDs, and reassurance", "Intra-articular corticosteroid injection", "Surgical excision of the ossicle", "Immobilization in a long leg cast for 6 weeks", "Strict non-weight bearing with crutches"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Osgood-Schlatter disease is a traction apophysitis of the tibial tubercle, common in active adolescents experiencing rapid growth. It is a self-limiting condition. Treatment is symptomatic with rest, ice, NSAIDs, and physical therapy (quadriceps/hamstring stretching).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 4-day-old infant is brought to the clinic for bilateral clubfoot (talipes equinovarus). The parents ask about the treatment plan. According to the Ponseti method, the deformities are corrected with serial casting in a specific sequence. Which component of the deformity is corrected LAST?', '["Adductus", "Cavus", "Supination", "Varus", "Equinus"]'::jsonb, 4, 'In the Ponseti method for clubfoot, the CAVE deformities are corrected sequentially: Cavus (elevating the first ray), Adductus, Varus, and finally Equinus. The equinus correction often requires a percutaneous Achilles tenotomy.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 5-year-old child refuses to bear weight on the right leg. The hip is held in flexion, abduction, and external rotation. Temperature is 39.0°C. Laboratory evaluation shows a WBC count of 15,000/mm³ and an ESR of 50 mm/hr. According to the Kocher criteria, what is the probability that this child has septic arthritis rather than transient synovitis?', '["Exactly 0%", "< 10% (Very low probability)", "About 25%", "> 90% (Very high probability)", "About 50%"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The Kocher criteria differentiate septic arthritis from transient synovitis. The 4 criteria are: non-weight-bearing, fever > 38.5°C, ESR > 40 mm/hr, and WBC > 12,000/mm³. Having all 4 predictors yields a 99% probability of septic arthritis; 3 predictors yield a 93% probability. This child has 4 predictors.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #52404: An 11-year-old overweight adolescent undergoes in situ pinning for a stable Slipped Capital Femoral Epiphysis (SCFE). At follow-up, the patient complains of severe stiffness and loss of joint space is noted on X-ray. What is this devastating complication?', '["Chondrolysis", "Avascular necrosis of the femoral head", "Nonunion", "Heterotopic ossification", "Infection"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Detailed orthopedic assessment of the pediatric patient is required to identify these specific conditions correctly and avoid serious long-term complications.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 4-year-old boy presents with a painless limp that has worsened over several weeks. He occasionally complains of vague right knee and thigh pain. He is afebrile and has limited internal rotation and abduction of the right hip. Radiographs show increased density and a subchondral crescent sign in the femoral head. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Slipped capital femoral epiphysis", "Transient synovitis", "Legg-Calv\u00e9-Perthes disease", "Developmental dysplasia of the hip", "Septic arthritis"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease is idiopathic avascular necrosis of the femoral head, classically seen in boys aged 4-8. It often presents insidiously with a painless limp or referred pain to the thigh/knee. Radiographs progress from sclerosis to fragmentation and reossification. The crescent sign represents a subchondral fracture.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 24-day-old infant is brought to the clinic for unilateral left clubfoot (talipes equinovarus). The parents ask about the treatment plan. According to the Ponseti method, the deformities are corrected with serial casting in a specific sequence. Which component of the deformity is corrected LAST?', '["Varus", "Cavus", "Supination", "Equinus", "Adductus"]'::jsonb, 3, 'In the Ponseti method for clubfoot, the CAVE deformities are corrected sequentially: Cavus (elevating the first ray), Adductus, Varus, and finally Equinus. The equinus correction often requires a percutaneous Achilles tenotomy.');
